        "depends on existing forms of social inequality both locally and globally"
      ],
      "confidence": 1.0
    },
    {
      "claim": "Hannah Arendt's The Human Condition abstracts from forms of inequality and hierarchy because she premised her work on the idea that postwar political emancipation, the welfare state, and capitalist abundance meant contemporary unfreedom involved society as a whole rather than the unjust rule of some over others.",
      "claim_type": "author_position",
      "supporting_excerpt": "Arendt tended to abstract from the forms of inequality and hierarchy that characterized postwar Europe and America, not to mention — and she didn’t— relations between the global North and the global South. ... Rather, her tendency to abstract in this way was the upshot of a speci ﬁc and hugely problematic premise about the shape of the postwar world, or at least of Arendt ’s corner of it: that the political emancipa-\ntion of the working class, the development of the welfare state, and the sheer\nabundance of the postwar capitalist economy meant that the forms of unfree-\ndom characteristic of the contemporary world no longer involved the unjust\nrule of some over others, but, as it were, a relation of society as a whole to each\nand all of its members.",
      "support_kind": "direct",
      "qualifications": [
        "characterizes Arendt's approach in The Human Condition"
      ],
      "confidence": 1.0
    }
  ],
